<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need a Lot of Books! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need a Lot of Books!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe a favorite book or movie. interactive: Engage in role-plays for everyday situations.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a favorite book or movie and ask peers for their opinion (e.g., 'I read an adventure book about treasure hunting. What do you think about adventure stories?' or 'I watched a comedy movie last weekend. Do you like comedies?'). Can participate in role-plays about common scenarios in the library (e.g., 'I need this book. Can I borrow it?'). Can exchange thoughts on familiar topics, like library activities or films (e.g., 'I liked the book; it was full of surprises.' 'The movie was exciting and fun to watch.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different types of books (fiction, non-fiction, adventure, comedy) and a picture of a library. The teacher asks students, "Do you like to read books?" "What kind of book do you like?" "Have you visited a library?" (Students answer chorally and individually with simple sentences.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences to describe books and library actions. "I read an adventure book last week. It was very interesting." (Teacher uses past simple). "Can I borrow this book?" (Teacher emphasizes intonation for the yes/no question). "You should be quiet in the library." (Teacher uses modal 'should'). Teacher explicitly points out short /ɪ/ in 'pick' and long /aɪ/ in 'find' by saying the words and asking students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "borrow": "If I borrow a book, do I keep it forever?" (No) "Do I need to return it later?" (Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "interesting": "Is an interesting book boring?" (No) "Does an interesting book make you want to read more?" (Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students or a student and a librarian in the library. The dialogue incorporates past simple (e.g., "I found a new book."), modals (e.g., "Can I borrow it?"), imperatives (e.g., "Go to the fiction section."), and vocabulary (library, book, shelf, librarian, interesting, check out, borrow, return, many).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in role-plays for everyday situations. Describe a favorite book or movie.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud. Students listen and then answer simple gist questions verbally in pairs:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who wants to borrow a book?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of book did the student like?" (e.g., "It was an adventure book.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where did they find the book?" (e.g., "On the shelf in the fiction section.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher also asks students to identify words with short /ɪ/ and long /aɪ/ sounds from the dialogue (e.g., 'pick', 'find').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using Past simple tense (I read/liked/found), Modals can/should (Can I borrow? You should be quiet), Imperatives (Go to the catalog), and library-related vocabulary. Focus on intonation for yes/no questions and identifying /ɪ/ and /aɪ/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice: Dialogue completion (Speaking focus):</b> Students work in pairs with a partial dialogue. They fill in the blanks orally using target grammar and vocabulary. E.g.,<br> A: "I ____ (read) a book last week."<br> B: "Oh, really? Was it ____ (interesting)?"<br> A: "Yes, it ____ (be) an adventure story. I really ____ (like) it."<br> B: "____ (Can) I ____ (borrow) it?"<br> Teacher monitors for correct past simple usage and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill: Pair questions and answers:</b> The teacher provides sentence frames. Students take turns asking and answering, substituting words from a list. <br> A: "What kind of book did you read?" <br> B: "I read a/an [adventure/fiction/non-fiction] book."<br> A: "Did you like the [story/characters/ending]?"<br> B: "Yes, I liked the [story]. It was [exciting/fun/surprising]." <br> Teacher reminds students to use correct intonation for questions (e.g., 'Can I take out this book?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative listing (Pragmatic function: Listing information):</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students collaboratively make a list of "Rules for the Library" and "Books We Want to Borrow." They use imperatives for rules (e.g., "Be quiet," "Return books") and "Can I borrow...?" for books. They share their lists orally. The teacher guides them to use 'many' or 'a lot' when talking about books.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "In the Library".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Role-play: "Visiting the Library"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair chooses one of two roles: Student or Librarian. They are given a specific scenario (e.g., Student wants to borrow a book they heard about, or Student needs help finding a book for research, or Student wants to describe an interesting book they just finished). They must include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asking to borrow a book: "Can I borrow this book?" (with correct intonation)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describing a book: "I read an interesting book about..." (using Past Simple)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Making a polite request: "You should look in the catalog." or "Could you help me find this title?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., library, book, catalog, author, interesting, checkout, shelf, return).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students perform their role-plays for another pair or the class. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency and communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their role-plays from Stage 4. Peers listen and provide feedback using a simple checklist provided by the teacher (e.g., "Did they ask 'Can I borrow...?'?", "Did they use library words?"). The teacher uses a checklist to observe students' speaking performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially intonation for questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., library, book, borrow, interesting).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Past Simple to describe preferences and actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of modals 'can' and 'should' and imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of communication and ability to engage in the role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new library words did I use today?" (List 3 words)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident am I now when asking to borrow a book in English? (Circle: Not confident / A little confident / Very confident)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when talking about books and the library today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of speaking in the library role-play do I want to practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The speaking focus was well-received, and most students actively participated in the role-plays. The Past Simple tense and modals 'can/should' were used with varying degrees of accuracy. Intonation for questions needs more explicit practice, perhaps through mimicry. Identifying short /ɪ/ and long /aɪ/ sounds was a good start, but students need more exposure and controlled practice to master them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Adjustments/Scaffolds needed:</b> Provide more sentence starters for the weaker students in the production stage. Integrate more visual cues for correct intonation. Create flashcards specifically targeting short and long vowel sounds for quick drills.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built confidence in spoken interactions about the library and books. In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the same vocabulary and grammar to write a short dialogue or a book recommendation/review, consolidating their understanding in a written format.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>